<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Latest news reports on FRANCE, French politics and culture - France 24</title><description>Follow FRANCE 24’s coverage of events in Paris and across France, Corsica and the French overseas territories. An insider look at French politics, culture, society, technology and sport through on-the-ground reports, investigations, exclusive interviews, debates and analyses.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/</link><lastBuildDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:37:48 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>French connections</category><title>Paris neighbourhoods: Exploring the lesser-known side of the 18th arrondissement</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/french-connections/20261029-paris-neighbourhoods-exploring-the-lesser-known-side-of-the-18th-arrondissement</link><description>
In this edition of French Connections, Genie Godula and Florence Villeminot continue their tour of Paris with a stop in the 18th arrondissement. The district is famous for landmarks like the Sacré-Cœur Basilica and the Moulin Rouge, but in this show we step away from the picture-postcard version of Montmartre and take you to see the rest of the 18th. We visit the vibrant multicultural neighbourhood called the Goutte d'Or, then take you to the largest fabric store in the world and introduce you to some of the 18th's most famous residents over the years. Join us as we explore the lesser-known side of the 18th arrondissement.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/49abb83e-cd58-11f1-b4a9-4da0d2c6855d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-MG-FRENCH-CONNECTIONS-18EME-ARRONDISSEMENT-v2-Sub-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/49abb83e-cd58-11f1-b4a9-4da0d2c6855d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-MG-FRENCH-CONNECTIONS-18EME-ARRONDISSEMENT-v2-Sub-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a985d26c-cd58-11f1-a3ca-0fe0d44bbfb3</guid><pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:30:47 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Florence VILLEMINOT</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>Paris shooting suspect admitted to targeting Muslims and Africans, prosecutor says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261029-live-french-anti-terrorism-prosecutor-to-give-press-conference-on-racist-paris-shooting</link><description>
A shooter suspected of killing one person and wounding five others, including a child, last week in the town of Trappes, near Paris, admitted to targeting Muslims and Africans, France’s anti-terrorism prosecutor said. The 39-year-old is set to be held in pre-trial detention and will appear before judges on Thursday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/783ae0c0-d385-11f1-b93e-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4T39XL.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/783ae0c0-d385-11f1-b93e-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4T39XL.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8bd91b28-d37c-11f1-9be7-6b57f45b3445</guid><pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:48:58 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/783ae0c0-d385-11f1-b93e-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4T39XL.jpg">© Anne-Christine Poujoulat, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Mbappé's China faux pas: When the sports world collides with the complexities of geopolitics</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-mbapp%C3%A9-s-china-faux-pas-when-the-sports-world-collides-with-the-complexities-of-geopolitics</link><description>
François Picard welcomes Igor Mladenovic, a French-Serbian sports writer, sports business analyst and former football commentator based in Luxembourg. For Mladenovic, the backlash in China over Kylian Mbappé's playful reference to a meme depicting him in the likeness of Mao Zedong illustrates a dilemma of the digital age: how a seemingly innocuous anecdote from a football superstar can escalate into a geopolitical firestorm.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/67f54040-d2e8-11f1-a593-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-42b27fae.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/67f54040-d2e8-11f1-a593-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-42b27fae.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e7b4ceac-d2c7-11f1-8831-1bcfcbfa86d6</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:17:34 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>French Senate backs 'make rioters pay' bill despite rights concerns</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261028-french-senate-backs-bill-to-make-protesters-pay-for-unrest-damage-despite-rights-concern</link><description>
France's Senate on Wednesday approved a bill authorising rioters to be held financially accountable for damage caused during social unrest, with penalties of up to €10,000 and possible welfare benefit seizures. Rights groups warn the legislation could undermine the public's fundamental right to protest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2c81769c-d26f-11f1-ab72-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/784d12b1ed74be1f6d5c41d953973c2ac4e1f8c4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2c81769c-d26f-11f1-ab72-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/784d12b1ed74be1f6d5c41d953973c2ac4e1f8c4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ffffcf66-d263-11f1-bebe-07a5fc99ccc4</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:10:35 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2c81769c-d26f-11f1-ab72-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/784d12b1ed74be1f6d5c41d953973c2ac4e1f8c4.jpg">© Thibaud Moritz, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>Rights groups say proposed French law to make rioters pay for damage is anti-democratic</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261027-rights-groups-say-proposed-french-law-to-make-rioters-pay-for-damage-anti-democratic</link><description>
Critics have said that a proposed French law that would make rioters liable to pay thousands of euros for any damage they cause is anti-democratic, as it could undermine the legitimate right to protest. The government put forward the law in a bid to quell looting and vandalism on the fringes of ongoing national protests over poor conditions in French schools and the high cost of living.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/49db0b42-d231-11f1-9ade-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26294363892004.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/49db0b42-d231-11f1-9ade-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26294363892004.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4fb3f650-d231-11f1-ab55-d72d8aeb94ab</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 18:27:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/49db0b42-d231-11f1-9ade-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26294363892004.jpg">© Teresa Suarez, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: Lawmakers hold a minute of silence for Pengo Bossio Wangi, victim of racist attack</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-france-lawmakers-hold-a-minute-of-silence-for-pengo-bossio-wangi-victim-of-racist-attack-1</link><description>
French lawmakers held a minute of silence for Pengo Bossio Wangi. The victim of a racist attack died in a shooting last week, while at least five other people were wounded, including a 12-years-old girl. FRANCE 24’s Claire Paccalin reports from the ground.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d26e3818-d226-11f1-a4c7-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-3b57773c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d26e3818-d226-11f1-a4c7-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-3b57773c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fa737d4c-d225-11f1-888e-9500dea4febf</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:08:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Entre Nous</category><title>Revisiting the delicate art of French patisserie</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/entre-nous/20261027-revisiting-the-delicate-art-of-french-patisserie</link><description>
In this edition of Entre Nous, we meet Nour Kandler, a self-taught pastry chef whose approach to French patisserie is shaped by a deep appreciation for its traditions and the rich flavours of her Middle Eastern heritage. She shares her unique perspective on pastry, where classic French techniques intertwine with cultural influences to create a style that is both personal and distinctive.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0e97c98e-d20a-11f1-bd76-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5758065c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0e97c98e-d20a-11f1-bd76-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5758065c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4ad10cc4-d207-11f1-80a1-5d84579d7797</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:45:37 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Annette YOUNG</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>French government concessions to students 'tactically shrewd' but risk creating new set of problems</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/french-government-concessions-to-students-tactically-shrewd-but-risk-creating-new-set-of-problems</link><description>
Oliver Farry welcomes Oliver Davis, Professor and Head of the Department of French at University College Cork. According to Professor Davis, the government's handling of the student protests reflects a combination of "tactical confusion" and an inadequate response to deeper structural problems. Davis cautions the concessions offered to secondary school students merely "scratch the surface." Meanwhile, France's widening protest movement has extended well beyond the classroom, drawing in the Yellow Vests, trade unions and people from across French society, amid mounting frustrations over public services and the rising cost of living.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4965e618-d1f6-11f1-82af-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261027-081020-081854-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4965e618-d1f6-11f1-82af-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261027-081020-081854-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4f1dc56c-d1f6-11f1-9959-adfc90f53f15</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:35:23 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>French education reform proposals 'vague': Govt created 'most of the problems' it now seeks to solve</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261027-french-education-reform-proposals-vague-govt-created-most-of-the-problems-it-now-seek-to-solve</link><description>
François Picard welcomes Jean-Rémi Girard, president of the SNALC teachers' union, who places responsibility for France's educational difficulties not simply on demographic change or fiscal constraints, but on years of political decisions that, he argues, have weakened the state education system. Against a backdrop of student protests, budgetary uncertainty and mounting concerns over educational inequality, Girard contends that the French government faces the formidable task of addressing a crisis largely of its own making.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c8e827b6-d219-11f1-9a22-bf69a9ce9852/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-22d492d2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c8e827b6-d219-11f1-9a22-bf69a9ce9852/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-22d492d2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7c85a736-d1d8-11f1-ab61-395d9b978d0a</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 07:31:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France protests: Macron rules out state of emergency – for now</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-france-protests-macron-rules-out-state-of-emergency-%E2%80%93-for-now</link><description>
The French government sought to draw a line Monday under weeks of protests that have been marred by violence, with President Emmanuel Macron heading to Marseille. France has been rocked by widespread anger over the rising cost of living, with nationwide demonstrations on October 17 degenerating into some of the country's worst rioting in years.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ceb15996-d157-11f1-9833-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/mini2-4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ceb15996-d157-11f1-9833-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/mini2-4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e8cbf1c4-d157-11f1-9921-91fcbe7069c1</guid><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:16:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Macron promises 'concrete measures' in response to French students' demands</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-french-president-promises-concrete-measures-in-response-to-french-students-demands</link><description>
French President Emmanuel Macron said the government would announce “concrete measures" on Monday (October 26) to address school students’ demands after weeks of unrest. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu was scheduled to meet with students later Monday. FRANCE 24's Luke Shrago reports from the ground.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f3422bda-cfbf-11f1-9c62-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4AX74W.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f3422bda-cfbf-11f1-9c62-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4AX74W.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">63087aba-d145-11f1-bc6a-9f07d106b8ee</guid><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:39:15 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>French student representatives hail govt's ‘concrete measures’, call for protests to continue</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-live-france-will-maintain-order-without-state-of-emergency-but-it-s-not-off-the-table-macron-says</link><description>
Representatives from French high school student groups on Monday hailed the government’s first measures to calm the crisis over ongoing unrest linked to their protest movement, including a gradual replacement of higher education admissions platform Parcoursup, but called for “continued mobilisation” after their meeting with Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu. Read about the day’s events as they unfolded on our liveblog. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5d44a936-d15a-11f1-a4c9-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D38V3ND.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5d44a936-d15a-11f1-a4c9-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D38V3ND.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f78756bc-d13a-11f1-96d4-a5bd40727198</guid><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:47:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5d44a936-d15a-11f1-a4c9-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D38V3ND.jpg">© Ludovic Marin, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>French students vow to continue protests despite concessions from Macron govt</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-macron-vows-concrete-measures-to-address-student-demands-after-unrest</link><description>
French student leaders on Monday called for more protests despite President Emmanuel Macron's pledge of  "concrete and strong" measures to address their demands after weeks of nationwide unrest. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu met with high school representatives alongside education ministers to announce follow-up action ranging from a total overhaul of higher education admissions platform Parcoursup to a cap on class sizes.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0691971e-d134-11f1-baaf-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299412584753.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0691971e-d134-11f1-baaf-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299412584753.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ade564d2-d134-11f1-9bc8-6d20ccb9a352</guid><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:25:21 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0691971e-d134-11f1-baaf-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299412584753.jpg">© Aurelien Morissard, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>French PM meets student representatives after weeks of protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/french-pm-meets-student-representatives-after-weeks-of-protests</link><description>
French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu is set to meet with high school students on Monday afternoon (October 26) to ease tensions in the country after nearly a month of protests. This follows another meeting in which French president Emmanuel Macron promised a concrete response to demands for better education standards and funding.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ded243ce-d136-11f1-ab58-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5e3192ff.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ded243ce-d136-11f1-ab58-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5e3192ff.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3964036a-d135-11f1-bc1d-5187f266c5d2</guid><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:20:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>Marine Le Pen's 'Renaissance': Fourth time lucky?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261025-le-pen-s-renaissance-4th-time-lucky</link><description>
Just before half past four this Sunday, Marine Le Pen took to the stage under the stadium strobe lights at the Zénith Arena in Orléans. The far-right French politician, who analysts say is on the doorstep of the presidency, is a polarising figure yet increasingly popular with her base, and has topped the popularity polls for months in France. Her entrance came 24 hours after the latest protests across the country, which included over 10,000 anti-Le Pen demonstrators outside the venue on Saturday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fb7fc8d0-d0a4-11f1-b748-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/WhatsApp-Image-2026-10-25-at-19-49-16.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fb7fc8d0-d0a4-11f1-b748-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/WhatsApp-Image-2026-10-25-at-19-49-16.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0e39f380-d099-11f1-809b-992c27e615cc</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 19:05:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Gavin LEE</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off</link><description>
Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest. Lecturer in Liberal Arts at Queen Mary University of London, Andrew Smith, shares further analysis.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/47c1def6-d094-11f1-bf55-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-171255-172709-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/47c1def6-d094-11f1-bf55-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-171255-172709-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4d7ba200-d094-11f1-a8d5-fd070f779b74</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:12:50 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>French far-right leader Le Pen kicks off presidential campaign</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/french-far-right-leader-le-pen-kicks-off-presidential-campaign</link><description>
We’ve just been listening to Marine Le Pen, leader of the far-right National Rally and its presidential candidate, delivering her first official campaign speech in Orléans.

She spoke of what she called a “renaissance” for France and the dawn of a new era. She also condemned what she described as “Islamist ideologies” that promote violence, saying they are incompatible with France.

Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan was listening in Orléans and joins us now with more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c855b27a-d091-11f1-be1a-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-170928-171254-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c855b27a-d091-11f1-be1a-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-170928-171254-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">cd60cf20-d091-11f1-b2f6-8539a710bce7</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:38:56 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: 1,314 arrested during protests across the country</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-1-314-arrested-during-protests-across-the-country</link><description>
France saw another day of nationwide protests on Saturday, with more than 400,000 people taking to the streets over the rising cost of living and school funding. Most marches remained peaceful, a week after riots and looting hit several cities. However, police say more than 1,300 people were arrested nationwide as violence broke out on the sidelines of demonstrations. Caroline Baum has more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7638bc76-d091-11f1-b532-bdb9616e85cf/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-160438-160623-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7638bc76-d091-11f1-b532-bdb9616e85cf/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-160438-160623-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7b9e8bb4-d091-11f1-8db5-55670c5cee14</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:32:57 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>French far-right leader Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-french-far-right-leader-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off</link><description>
Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4821683a-d094-11f1-ab22-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26298602550298.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4821683a-d094-11f1-ab22-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26298602550298.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8f52891c-d07d-11f1-aca6-a965235ed70c</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:01:51 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4821683a-d094-11f1-ab22-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26298602550298.jpg">AP Photo/Teresa Suarez - Teresa Suarez</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: 110,000 protesters in Paris</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-110-000-protesters-in-paris</link><description>
Hundreds of demonstrations took place across France on Saturday over the rising cost of living, with security forces deployed in large numbers to prevent a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. While the marches themselves “passed off well” in several cities, Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez said members of “ultra-left” groups threw projectiles and fireworks at police in an attempt to provoke clashes. French Communist Party spokesperson Fatima Khallouk shares more details.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b799d560-d07d-11f1-abd8-bdb9616e85cf/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261024-180607-181821-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b799d560-d07d-11f1-abd8-bdb9616e85cf/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261024-180607-181821-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bcc1df24-d07d-11f1-9fef-37dd3010ebf0</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:00:01 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Calais, 10 years after the Jungle: Migrants still risk the Channel crossing</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-calais-10-years-after-the-jungle-migrants-still-risk-the-channel-crossing</link><description>
Ten years ago, in 2016, thousands of #migrants lived in what was known as the “Great #Jungle”, a vast camp on the outskirts of #Calais. It took the authorities seven days to dismantle it.
 
Today, dozens of smaller “jungles” are scattered across a wider stretch of the coastline. These camps are dismantled every 48 hours on average.
 
For migrants, the goal remains the same: to cross the Channel and reach the United Kingdom. Despite the risks posed by “small boats”, these makeshift vessels, many continue to attempt the crossing in the hope of a better life.
 
Our reporters Pauline Godart, Claire Paccalin and Mohamed Farhat travelled to northern France for #BilletRetour.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ae9a830-d084-11f1-9697-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/Screenshot-2026-10-25-alle-15-58-03.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ae9a830-d084-11f1-9697-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/Screenshot-2026-10-25-alle-15-58-03.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d2119dc8-d07e-11f1-8151-2f7ad65100a7</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:17:32 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Le Pen leads polls with up to 38% six months before election</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/le-pen-leads-polls-with-up-to-38-six-months-before-election</link><description>
The day after the first day of the National Rally congress in Orléans, in central France, passed without any major incidents, Marine Le Pen is due to deliver her first major campaign speech on Sunday afternoon, 25 October, under heavy police surveillance. The speech, centred on the theme of renewal, aims to inject a dose of optimism six months ahead of the presidential election, the candidate has reportedly said in private.
 
Gilles Ivaldi, a political science researcher at the French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS) and the University of Nice, explains that the violence following protests across France in recent weeks could help the National Rally present itself as the party capable of restoring public safety and national security.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d60c29ce-d07a-11f1-9881-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26297524255923.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d60c29ce-d07a-11f1-9881-4d317af013fd/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26297524255923.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">73ca095c-d07a-11f1-ba4c-f3ad5062cd50</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:51:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: Le Pen launches presidential campaign in Orleans</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-launches-presidential-campaign-in-orleans</link><description>
Far-right leader Marine Le Pen is launching her presidential campaign today. The National Rally candidate is due to deliver her first campaign speech in Orléans, addressing around 10,000 party members. Police were controlling traffic entering the city this morning, following a large anti-far-right demonstration on Saturday, where thousands of protesters marched chanting anti-fascist slogans.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/53eb1ff0-d079-11f1-9876-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-130201-130355-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/53eb1ff0-d079-11f1-9876-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-130201-130355-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">590f2e22-d079-11f1-a8cc-0d2d32657c0b</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:46:08 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Far-right leader Le Pen says visible policing keeps protests smooth</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/far-right-leader-le-pen-says-visible-policing-keeps-protests-smooth</link><description>
Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan has been following the latest developments.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5160b9a4-d068-11f1-b673-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-121058-121405-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5160b9a4-d068-11f1-b673-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-121058-121405-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">56cbc668-d068-11f1-a57b-25b3852b0c3f</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:39:18 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>At least 1,300 people arrested in mass weekend protests across France</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-at-least-1-300-people-arrested-in-mass-weekend-protests-across-france</link><description>
At least 1,300 people were arrested on the sidelines of Saturday's protests across France and at least 50 officers and three protesters were hurt, the interior ministry said Sunday. The arrests occurred as clashes broke out on the margins of largely calm demonstrations in major cities over the high cost of living.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b7dd62bc-d05a-11f1-88d3-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26297559777844.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b7dd62bc-d05a-11f1-88d3-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26297559777844.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1061870a-d061-11f1-a8d9-274107ab047a</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:02:41 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b7dd62bc-d05a-11f1-88d3-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26297559777844.jpg">© Jean-Francois Badias, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>Suspect in racist Paris shooting transferred to psychiatric unit, had drugs in his system</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-suspect-in-racist-gun-attack-near-paris-transferred-to-psychiatric-unit</link><description>
A shooter suspected of killing one person and wounding five others on Friday in the town of Trappes, near Paris, has had his police custody suspended for a 48-hour psychiatric observation, anti-terror prosecutors said Sunday. The 39-year-old man is believed to have had racist motivations behind the gun attack as most of the victims had foreign origins.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a69c99f4-d05b-11f1-b633-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D47R7WB.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a69c99f4-d05b-11f1-b633-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D47R7WB.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">01ee3626-d05e-11f1-a0cf-9b2783ed58d1</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 10:36:42 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a69c99f4-d05b-11f1-b633-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D47R7WB.jpg">© Dimitar Dilkoff, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France: Some clashes erupted at end of protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-some-clashes-erupted-at-end-of-protests</link><description>
There were some clashes on Saturday, mainly in major cities and as demonstrations came to an end. French authorities say police made nearly 1,000 arrests nationwide. Here’s a report from our sister channel, France 2.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/913459ee-d056-11f1-8548-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-090423-090631-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/913459ee-d056-11f1-8548-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-090423-090631-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">96ec49f0-d056-11f1-91a9-9db82fb1c219</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:35:20 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>More than 400.000 protest across France</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/more-than-400-000-protest-across-france</link><description>
Saturday’s demonstrations across France were largely peaceful, easing fears of a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. Hundreds of thousands took to the streets to demand action on the rising cost of living and more funding for public services, with students, workers and retirees marching side by side. Many say their anger shows no sign of fading. The government put turnout at 410,000 nationwide, while the CGT union estimated the figure was almost twice as high. Here’s Jack Pownall with more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4fb7d9d2-d056-11f1-905d-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-090147-090407-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4fb7d9d2-d056-11f1-905d-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-090147-090407-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">54b08f1a-d056-11f1-b07c-81e176334a8f</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:32:21 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>France’s far right gathers as Le Pen prepares her presidential campaign</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-s-far-right-gathers-as-le-pen-prepares-her-presidential-campaign</link><description>
Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. For more on what’s happening there, let’s cross to our reporter, Antonia Kerrigan.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4f38fff0-d055-11f1-a349-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-100832-101312-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4f38fff0-d055-11f1-a349-bba4eee3dc72/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-100832-101312-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">54b8bb14-d055-11f1-93d0-910454473283</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:31:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Hundreds of thousands march in France to protest against government</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/hundreds-of-thousands-march-in-france-to-protest-against-government</link><description>
Hundreds of thousands marched across France on Saturday protesting the rising cost of living and a lack of government resources for essential services like schools. While demonstrations in Paris started peacefully they ended with a heavy security response, with almost 1,000 arrested across the country. France 24 International Affairs Editor Oliver Farry shares further details.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c1540d4-d055-11f1-ad06-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-100212-100803-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c1540d4-d055-11f1-ad06-4b25c96ec90e/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261025-100212-100803-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">212df264-d055-11f1-8954-1f5a35e6fa2e</guid><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:29:39 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
